WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.250
The Walking Dead has been a whirlwind success
for AMC, and its continued high ratings are

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.930
proof that the recent zombie pop culture craze
is more than a fad.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:13.070
But the show has not been without its fair
share of issues, both on-screen and off.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.310
Here's the behind-the-scenes history of The
Walking Dead you might not know.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.580
Beam me up, Robby

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.609
When comic book writer Robert Kirkman first
came up with his concept for The Walking Dead,

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:27.980
he didn't want his idea to fester in some
pile of generic zombie pitches, so he added

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.650
a little something extra to the mix: aliens.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.910
Yes, aliens.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.070
Kirkman revealed that when he first approached
publisher Image Comics about his story ideas,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.980
they thought his story needed an extra hook,
so he threw in an otherworldly element, suggesting

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.570
that his zombies could have been sent to Earth
by aliens.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.650
Kirkman never intended to follow through with
that plot track, but it got him the green

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.650
light.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:56.050
Ultimately, Kirkman did circle back on the
alien theme in Issue #75, which featured a

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:00.100
bonus ending that had some fun with that original
concept.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.090
Artist bites back

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:06.039
Tony Moore, whose artwork appeared in many
early issues of Kirkman's comic series, filed

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:10.060
suit against the author — his childhood
friend — after he felt duped into signing

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.530
over his financial rights to the franchise.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.219
He claimed he was owed a significant slice
of the brainy pie once the series hit it big.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:21.029
Kirkman disagreed and filed a counter-suit
against Moore, alleging he'd breached their

00:01:21.029 --> 00:01:23.659
confidentiality agreement with his filing.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:27.789
The two amicably settled their dispute in
2012, and Moore returned to draw a variant

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:33.799
cover for The Walking Dead Issue #150, paying
tribute to his cover of issue #1, which debuted

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:36.109
back in 2003.

00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:37.829
An intro to remember

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:40.979
A lot of things have changed over the years
on The Walking Dead, but one thing that's

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:45.389
remained the same is that spooky intro music
that lulls us into some undead submission

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:48.169
every episode.

00:01:48.169 --> 00:01:57.919
(spooky intro music playing)

00:01:57.919 --> 00:02:02.119
Composer Bear McCreary designed the iconic
score, and yes, he intended for it to be just

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:03.259
that dreary.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:07.179
(live spooky intro music playing)

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:13.810
In a 2010 interview with MTV News, he explained
that he wanted to capture a sense of "emptiness"

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.280
with the stringed notes of his opening number,
as it was the feeling he got from watching

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.280
it.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.210
He said, "I wanted to create something that
would get stuck in your head, so that when

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.980
you hear it, you would think of The Walking
Dead."

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.390
Mission accomplished.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.510
Showrunner switcheroo

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.049
Frank Darabont was The Walking Dead's first
showrunner, and his fingerprints are still

00:02:32.049 --> 00:02:33.379
all over the place.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:37.511
Not only did he recruit the original cast,
some of whom he'd worked with before, but

00:02:37.511 --> 00:02:42.019
he built the world foundation upon which the
show still stands with Robert Kirkman.

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:45.879
So it was a complete shock to him and everyone
else when the network decided to give him

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:47.660
the boot in Season 2.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:52.340
Darabont pursued legal action against AMC,
accusing the network of creating "crisis-level

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.251
problems" by slashing the show's budget in
spite of ratings success and accused executives

00:02:57.251 --> 00:02:59.719
of essentially inventing reasons to fire him.

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:04.890
As of the making of this video, Darabont is
seeking more than $280 million in damages.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:09.079
Jeffrey DeMunn, who'd starred in a ton of
Darabont's movies, including Shawshank Redemption,

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:13.260
The Green Mile, and The Mist, loyally followed
Darabont through the exit sign soon after

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.569
his untimely departure.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:17.870
Pre-Daryl Dixon

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.860
Norman Reedus' crossbow-wielding hero Daryl
Dixon has emerged as one of the show's fan-favorite

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.999
characters, but Reedus originally auditioned
for the part of Merle, which had already gone

00:03:26.999 --> 00:03:28.620
to Michael Rooker.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.209
Showrunners liked Reedus so much, though,
they created the part of Daryl specifically

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:33.209
for him.

00:03:33.209 --> 00:03:48.230
Talk about a happy accident.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.629
The jail scene

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.519
The prison setting might have been fictional,
but some of The Walking Dead's cast members

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:58.069
have found themselves behind bars in real-life
for their...extracurricular activities.

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:03.280
In 2012, actor Scott Wilson, who played old
man Herschel in the show, was arrested for

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.280
drunk driving.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.969
Then in 2015, Seth Gilliam, who plays the
good Reverend Gabriel, was also arrested for

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:11.130
DUI and possession of marijuana.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.170
Sheriff Rick must have been off duty those
nights.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.150
Thanks, Carol

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:20.959
Steven Yeun was born in Seoul, South Korea,
so his parents have something of a language

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:24.250
barrier when it comes to fully understanding
what's happening on the show.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:28.020
Apparently, in a moment of confusion, they
told him to make sure and thank co-star Melissa

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.569
McBride for that time Carol saved everyone
from the cannibals.

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:35.419
Even after Yeun tried to explain that everything
happening on the show is fictional, his father

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:38.610
insisted he thank her, so he did.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.610
Hero dog

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:44.910
Dooley, the dog who played Daryl and Beth's
one-eyed canine companion in Season 4, was

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.389
a real-life hero.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:49.599
He lost his right eye while defending his
owner from a carjacker.

00:04:49.599 --> 00:04:51.630
Bravest cast-member ever?

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:52.800
Yup!

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.599
Zombie makeup levels

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:58.430
With so many zombified extras at play in any
given episode, the makeup department has had

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:02.980
to be choosy as to which members of its crowd
get the full work-up, so execs devised three

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.250
levels of zombies.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.490
The highest is hero, which gets the full body
makeover … like this guy.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:12.930
"Make sure we slime the back of his head and
shoulders really well."

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:21.819
Then there's midground, which warrants some
facial transformation and costuming.

00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:25.460
And when there's a horde of zombies on-screen,
many of the undead extras further away from

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.710
the camera just wear a zombie mask and some
ragged clothing.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.639
No matter the level, though, every extra has
to go to zombie school before filming to learn

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:34.340
how to move like a true biter.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.340
(groaning)

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.340
"What is that?"

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.340
"What about yours?"

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.340
"Ay?"

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.510
"Who died and made you f***ing king of the
zombies?"

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:42.520
"What?

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.530
Oh"

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:44.540
(groaning)

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.280
"Yeah, that's pretty good."

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.280
Thanks for watching!

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.069
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw, and let

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:53.830
us know in the comments which Walking Dead
fact surprised you the most...

